{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2495","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2495","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2495\",\"parent_revision\":\"a73010f0191207aed0cc3a6994ca2910c9f1bda3f7bf3f1049202c39c99ae144\",\"root_revision\":\"a73010f0191207aed0cc3a6994ca2910c9f1bda3f7bf3f1049202c39c99ae144\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_view` with `summary=false` exceeds the 4000-token budget on large GitHub-backed items and returns only metadata fields (title, priority, status, description) \u2014 the body sections are not returned. When section filters are applied (`section='N'`, `section='/regex/'`, `section='Name'`), the response reports `section_filter_miss: true` even though the sections exist. The only workaround is reading the local YAML cache file directly (`~/.dh/projects/.../backlog/*.yaml`).\\n\\nObserved during grooming of #2438 (body size ~33706 chars). All four filter forms were attempted and failed: `section=4`, `section=13`, `section='RT-ICA'`, `sections=['RT-ICA','Issue Classification']`. The over-budget flag appears to suppress body section delivery before the section filter is applied.\\n\\nImpact: agents and orchestrators cannot read specific groomed sections from large items via MCP; must fall back to local filesystem reads which bypass the MCP abstraction layer and fail in worktree-isolated agents.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [x] `_apply_body_section_filter` supports all four filter forms matching `_filter_sections` behaviour:\\n  - Numeric index (`'2'`)\\n  - Comma-separated indices (`'0,2,4'`)\\n  - Regex (`'/pattern/'`)\\n  - Substring match (any other string \u2014 `'RT-ICA'` matches `## RT-ICA Analysis`)\\n- [x] `section='RT-ICA'` on a GitHub item with header `## RT-ICA Analysis` returns the section content, not `section_filter_miss: True`\\n- [x] 13 regression tests cover all four filter forms (`backlog_core/tests/test_apply_body_section_filter_forms.py`)\\n- [x] Removes false docstring claim \\\"no effect on GitHub-only items with a raw body\\\" from `server.py`\\n- [x] `prek` clean; full `backlog_core/tests/` suite 114/114 pass\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want **`backlog_view` section filters to work correctly on large GitHub-backed items** so that **agents can read specific sections from large issues without falling back to direct filesystem reads**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Feature-researcher concerns block during grooming of #2438, session 2026-05-30\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-30\\n- **Fixed**: 2026-05-30 (PR #2501)\\n- **Related debt**: Module size (#2498) was surfaced as pre-existing context for this bug\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root Cause\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Design bug**: `_apply_body_section_filter` (raw-body GitHub item path, `operations.py:2726`) used exact case-insensitive header-name matching (`==`). `_filter_sections` (YAML item path) uses four forms: numeric index, comma-separated indices, `/regex/`, and substring match.\\n\\nResult: `section='RT-ICA'` on a GitHub item with `## RT-ICA Analysis` set `section_filter_miss=True` (exact match fails). Full body stayed in `result.body`, exceeded the 16 000-char budget threshold, and `backlog_view` returned the compact over-budget manifest instead of the requested section.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPR #2501 \u2014 `fix(development-harness): section filter parity for raw-body GitHub items`\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"bc9f387e8198059295b480f9f44a7dd196200a7b3f5e7da3fa2b90fe9edc2e21\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCOIgA\"}"}